# Worked Example: Splitter's Imperative Bus Command

`<Splitter>` (CORE.md §2, point 2) has no controlled prop and no ref for
its own split ratio — `initialSplit` only ever seeds the *starting*
position. The only way to command an already-mounted Splitter from
outside is `aiBus.emit('splitter:split_changed', { id, split })`, matched
against the Splitter instance's own `id`. This walks through why that's
the right shape, not a missing prop.

## The problem: reaching for state that doesn't exist

```tsx
// Don't do this — there's no ref API to reach for.
const splitterRef = useRef<{ setSplit: (n: number) => void }>(null);

<Button onClick={() => splitterRef.current?.setSplit(100)}>
  Collapse panel
</Button>
<Splitter id="main-layout" ref={splitterRef} orientation="vertical">
  <Splitter.Panel>{/* ... */}</Splitter.Panel>
  <Splitter.Panel>{/* ... */}</Splitter.Panel>
</Splitter>
```

`Splitter` doesn't forward a ref for this at all — there's nothing to
imperatively call, by design, not by omission.

## The fix: command it over the bus, by id

```tsx
import { aiBus, Splitter, Button } from '#toolcrib';

<Button onClick={() => aiBus.emit('splitter:split_changed', { id: 'main-layout', split: 100 })}>
  Collapse panel
</Button>
<Splitter id="main-layout" orientation="vertical">
  <Splitter.Panel>{/* ... */}</Splitter.Panel>
  <Splitter.Panel>{/* ... */}</Splitter.Panel>
</Splitter>
```

`id` is what scopes the command to this specific Splitter instance, the
same matching pattern every other id-addressed bus channel in the toolkit
uses (`aiBus.openModal(id, ...)`, `TabStrip`'s `groupId`). The channel's
exact payload shape is:

```ts
{{splitterSplitChangedPayload}}
```

## Why this isn't just a weaker version of a ref

A ref would make this a one-way remote control — call it once, and
nothing keeps the caller's idea of "the split" in sync with what the user
actually does next. The bus command doesn't have that problem, because
it's the same channel the Splitter's own internal drag/keyboard handling
already emits on every real change — an external `emit` and a user
dragging the handle both funnel through the identical path. Concretely:
a mismatched `id` is silently ignored (no risk of one Splitter's button
accidentally moving a different one), an out-of-range `split` clamps
through the same `minSize`/`maxSize` logic a drag already respects, and a
command that arrives *from* the bus never re-emits back onto it — so two
Splitters (or a Splitter and an external listener) can't loop each other.
Unlike `tab:changed`, this channel is **not sticky** — a late-mounting
listener won't get the current split replayed to it, since there's no
mount-order race to solve here the way `TabStrip`/`TabStrip.Panel`'s
cross-tree relationship has (see the event-bus-sticky-replay example) —
this is a same-tree, imperative command, not cross-tree state sync.
